and now as emily was just saying of  course today is  remembering sunday and the covert  restrictions mean that many won't be  able to mark this most important day in  the way that they would you  usually would and would certainly want  to but of course it's still  a time to reflect on the immense  sacrifice of one generation  to mark the occasion we had an interview  this week with the chief of the defense  staff senate carter i was busy with our  u.s election coverage so my  top colleague debra haynes our foreign  affairs editor went along to speak  to the head of the british armed forces  it's a unique time the country  under lock down an economic crisis that  we haven't known for for generations  like why should  people care about remembrance well i  think the most important thing about it  is that it's about honouring those who  gave their lives  in the service of our country and of  course they did that to protect our way  of life and our freedom  and i think it would be very dangerous  if we forgot that not least i think  because  you know we need to hang on to that idea  of democracy  why is that dangerous if we don't  remember the sacrifice of the past  well because i think that what we will  also forget is the true horror of war  and if you forget about the horror of  war then the great risk i think is that  people might think that going to war is  a reasonable thing to do  now in certain circumstances that might  be the case but of course you need to  understand what you're taking on when  you do it  because it's always visceral it's always  violent it's always brutal  and ultimately it leads to people having  to sacrifice their sacrifice their lives  in the in the cause and that's a you  know quite a thing for people to do  so we need to remember that we need to  learn from it and we need to make sure  that  people only take us to war when they  absolutely have to and like looking at  history  history tells us that economic crises  lead to security crises how worried are  you that the  global economic crisis brought on by  coronavirus  could lead to new security threats  potentially even war  yes i mean i think you know we're living  at a moment in time where the world is a  very uncertain  and anxious place and of course the  dynamic of  global competition uh is a feature of  our lives as well  and i think the real risk we have with  quite a lot of the regional conflicts  that are going on at the moment  is you could see escalation lead to  miscalculation  and that's the thing i think we have to  guard against miscalculation can you  just spell that out in  easy language what does that mean so  what that means is is that the  protagonists either because they don't  realize the implications of their  actions  lead to an escalation which means that  more people perhaps get involved more  weaponry gets involved  and before you can contain it it leads  to  the sides ending up in a full-blown war  and if that happens as we saw in the  last  century i mean we have to in the history  might not repeat itself it has a rhythm  and if you look back at the last century  before both world wars i think it was  unarguable that there was escalation  that led to the miscalculation which  ultimately led to  war at a scale we would hopefully never  see again so are you saying that's a  real threat that there could be another  world war i'm saying it's a risk  and i think we need to be conscious of  those risks and that's why remembrance  matters because  if you look back at history hopefully  you learn from their experience and you  make sure that you're very cautious  about how you manage the sorts of  regional conflicts that we see  playing out in the world today um  looking at coronavirus  uh the first time around it seemed that  the government was kind of almost  wary reluctant to use the military and  the armed forces being involved on the  streets may be a sign that  things aren't going so well was that  frustrating for you  and do you think that this time around  in the second wave  we'll see more even more military  presence obviously the military have  already done a lot  um i don't think the government was in  any way reluctant to use the armed  forces  i think it's always a question about  what the armed forces can do  and of course over the last 10 years  we've seen a lot of times that the armed  forces have been deployed  in support of the civil authority  whether it was the 2012  olympics which you'll remember but  equally there's been a lot of bad  weather  over the course of the last 10 years and  often we're deployed in support of  flooding now i don't think the  government has ever felt that it was  inappropriate to do  that or indeed it showed that things  have gone wrong and the answer is that  the armed forces are there to provide  that level of national resilience so i  didn't think there was any  reluctance to use the armed forces i  think like all these things it's about  working out  how you can use the armed forces in the  most effective way  and actually i've been hugely proud of  what we've contributed  you know we've done it behind the scenes  we've been supporting  the health workers on the front line and  we've applied some quite imaginative  ideas to how we can help them  and i think what we did with  constructing the nightingale hospitals  which  sadly look like they'll be back in use  again what we've done in terms of some  quite imaginative work  in the testing program and indeed what  we did to deliver  ppe the personal protection equipment at  scale  all of that i think are indications of  how the armed forces can  turn their hands to most things when  they're required to as part of that  national resilience  and we've been told by the defense  secretary that there are seven and a  half thousand  military personnel um ready as part of  this winter defense force  can you imagine all of them potentially  having to be used  over the coming months well of course we  always aim off for contingencies  um and um we're aiming off for all sorts  of things that might happen this winter  not least  bad weather so the answer is i don't  know um but we are prepared for whatever  eventuality might happen  and of course we're about to deploy um  2000 or so  people up to liverpool to take part in  this multi-testing  pilot scheme that's underway and i  suspect that if that works successfully  we might find that there are other areas  where we need to help in a similar sort  of fashion  so test and trace it's it's a key  fundamentally a big logistical challenge  isn't that exactly what the armed forces  are designed to be able to achieve  wouldn't it make much more sense just to  give that whole  piece of important work to the military  and let them run the command and control  and run that test and trace program  well i mean it is an option but i think  at the moment we should um  have confidence in the testing system i  mean when you think back in may  we were only able to do 10 000 tests a  day  we're now doing 500 000 tests a day and  that is on a very steep  path at the moment and i think actually  the testing thing has been a really  impressive effort  the trace thing is another matter  altogether and i'm not sure that that's  necessarily  a military task of course we'll step up  to the plate if we're invited to do it  but i've got confidence in the system at  the moment but you say it's an option  like have you ever offered the military  up to be this  solution to help the country deal with  the challenge of  of having an effective testing program i  think we've always been  clear in defense that we're available  and the government's always known we are  um ultimately you want sustainable  solutions and what we must never lose  sight of of course is that our principal  purpose is to  deter the country's enemies and we do  that on an ongoing basis  and whilst it is entirely appropriate  for us to help in times of crisis  what the government really needs are  sustainable solutions so that we're  available to do what  we should be doing on a normal daily  basis well that leads us on very nicely  to  the integrated review obviously there's  this big review going on that's been  going on for months now of  of uk defense foreign uh development  policy um for you uh how  important or how hopeful are you that  that defense will be given  um a a multi-year settlement from the  treasury even though obviously we've  heard from the treasury that  most of the departments will be only  getting a one-year budget i mean the  answer is i i don't know  um at the moment there are negotiations  going on in a very constructive way  we've absolutely we absolutely  understand the challenge the country has  and we've talked in this interview about  the  very difficult fiscal situation and of  course from the  treasury's perspective you know you need  to be sure of your fiscal assumptions  before you're clear about whatever the  future might hold and therefore what a  long-term settlement might look like  but clearly from our perspective we're  going to argue for something like that  because  we need long-term investment because  long-term investment gives us the  opportunity to have confidence in  modernisation  you know modernization essentially means  that you're going to park some  capabilities that are perhaps from the  industrial age  and want to look forward to some of the  capabilities you need for an information  age  and having the confidence to do that of  course means you need confidence in your  long-term investment i mean on that  front  should the country be ready when they  look at their military um to have  a very different looking armed forces  going forwards  they're actually fixating on the size of  the armed forces  on this 82 000 strong army is actually  the wrong metric and what you should be  looking  at is capability increased use of  autonomous weapons  increased use of cyber of space to be  able to have maximum effect  are we going to see a smaller armed  forces but are more powerful armed  forces  um i think you're going to see an armed  forces that's designed for  the 2030s i mean you won't get there  overnight but that's the direction of  travel  and we think that a armed force is  designed for then is going to be one  that  is organized through the prism of the  domains of land are maritime but also  cyber and space because it's the way  that you integrate all of those domains  together  these days and certainly into the future  that gives you your disruptive edge  so i think you'll see armed forces that  are designed to do that and i think that  absolutely means that  we will have all manner of different  people employed because those domains  require different skill sets  and we will absolutely avail ourselves  of autonomous platforms  and robotics wherever we can but we  don't yet know and nobody can predict  the future  quite what mix of technologies will be  necessary  to be able to achieve that and that's  why returning to  the lost art of experimentation  something that perhaps we haven't done  constructively since the 20 the 1930s is  something that we'll absolutely have to  do  but can you spell that out does that  mean like the 82 000 figure is going to  go and it's not going to it's going to  be a smaller  actual military no i can't spell that  out not least because decisions haven't  been taken  but what i'm hinting at is that we need  to be thinking about how we measure  effect in a different way  i mean i suspect we could have an army  of 120 000 at which  30 000 might be robots who knows but the  answer is we need to open our minds  to perhaps numbers not determining what  we should be doing but rather the effect  that we can achieve  is really what we should be looking for  the uk threat level has risen we've seen  terror attacks  in austria and in france is this a sign  of a  resurgent islamic state a resurgent  islamist extremist threat that could  play out here in the uk too  i mean the answer is i don't know and of  course our thoughts are absolutely with  um  those who've been affected in france and  of course in austria um  the plain fact is that the challenge of  um daish  of the islamic state has not gone away  and we have forces deployed in parts of  the world at the moment which are  dealing with that particular challenge  uh it's a clear and present threat  and it's one that we should all be alert  to the overseas operations bill  um is obviously passing through  parliament at the moment  and you'll you'll be fully aware of the  concerns that have been raised  and particularly by by labor and also by  a number of conservative mps that this  is giving um a carte blanche to the uk  armed forces  to commit war crimes potentially torture  what do you say to that i mean my  starting point would always be  that if uh our forces um  do not behave in line with our values  and standards and the laws of war  on the ground then they should be  properly held to account for that  but i would also say that we have been  struggling with that  vexatious claims from the campaigns of  iraq and afghanistan over the last  decade or two and that is something  which i can't condone  because ultimately that threatens the  way in which our leaders  on the ground are prepared to seize  those fleeting opportunities that you  need to win on a battlefield  so the trick is to maintain the moral  high ground which is fundamental to us  we must never lose that  and therefore we must be held to account  whilst at the same time trying to make  sure that  vexatious claims don't pursue us okay  thank you very much

